le": "The fundamentals baseline (-7.5) is reinforced by Strada's dominant fundraising, the R+7 partisan lean, and a Likely/Solid R expert consensus; the lone tied poll is old, partisan-sponsored, and statistically negligible. I nudge the margin slightly toward Republicans for the money gap while keeping uncertainty wide given the open seat and thin polling.", "watch": "A late Democratic-funded polling surge or national environment shifting several points could move this toward Lean R.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 56 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Open seat: Daniel Webster (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "This central Florida open seat, held by retiring Republican Daniel Webster, leans Republican by about seven points on partisanship. Joe Strada survived a recount to win the GOP nomination and has raised over $5 million, dwarfing Democrat James Pericola's roughly $120,000. A Libertarian is also on the ballot.\n\nExpert forecasters uniformly rate the race Likely R or better, and prediction markets give the Democrat about a 12% chance. The only poll, a July survey sponsored by a Democratic-leaning firm, showed a tie, but it is dated and carries little weight. The fundamentals point to a Republican win in the mid-to-high single digits, with modest residual uncertainty from the open-seat dynamic and sparse polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1071, "margin": -7.48}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0948, "margin": -9.0}]}